WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.400
hello and welcome, i'm james murphy
and today i'm going to show you why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.360
every proof that you've seen
of the fact that 0.99 repeating equals 1 is wrong.&nbsp;

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:13.520
don't worry, it IS true.
this isn't one of those videos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.000
it's just the proofs that&nbsp;
you've seen that are wrong

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.440
so first i'll show you why&nbsp;
those other proofs are wrong,&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.920
and then i'll show you my actual proof.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:28.480
the vast majority of proofs that .99 repeating&nbsp;
equals one are so-called algebraic proofs&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.320
they start with some number,
they do some algebraic manipulations,&nbsp;

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:36.880
and then they somehow derive that one&nbsp;
is equal to 0.99 repeating from that.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.800
these are by far the most common kind of&nbsp;
proof that you'll see when you look it up&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.120
and at first glance they look pretty sensible.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:48.160
i start with x being 0.99&nbsp;
repeating then i multiply by 10,&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.880
i subtract x and then i solve for&nbsp;
x equals 1. seems valid right?

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.520
we all know a third is 0.33 repeating,&nbsp;

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:59.600
multiply by 3 and then we get&nbsp;
1 is 0.99 repeating, right?

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.360
well it turns out there are&nbsp;
multiple mistakes in these proofs.&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.880
well right at the very start we've&nbsp;
actually made a huge mistake.&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.920
just by writing 0.99 repeating, i'm&nbsp;
implicitly assuming that it exists.&nbsp;

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.320
if .99 repeating didn't exist&nbsp;
then i wouldn't be able to&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.640
multiply it by 10 and then solve for it.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:21.440
this is the error that pretty&nbsp;
much every video makes.&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:24.640
i've only seen one that doesn't make this mistake.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.560
let me show you what can go wrong in&nbsp;
a very similar situation by making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.600
this assumption that it does exist.
instead of looking at 0.99 repeating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.800
let's look at a very similar expression.
let's suppose that a number 9 repeating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.520
and then the point exists.
so i'm thinking here as the decimal point&nbsp;

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.480
with nines extending out on the left side&nbsp;
of it instead of on the right side of it.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:54.000
i start with 9 repeating, i multiply by&nbsp;
10, i add 9, and i see that i end up back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.680
with the number that i started with.
so then i can solve for that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.040
algebraically and i get that x is minus 1.
pause the video and check the steps if you want.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:06.320
so do you believe that 9 repeating point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.920
is equal to negative one?
well no you really shouldn't.&nbsp;

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:16.320
there are number systems where this kind of thing&nbsp;
is true, but the real numbers is not one of them.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:20.960
okay so suppose you're willing to believe&nbsp;
that it does exist as a real number&nbsp;

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.760
then the rest of the proof works right?
well still no. so what's wrong? is it one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.960
of the highlighted green statements?
which one of those is wrong?&nbsp;

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.840
well, none of them.
all of those statements are true.&nbsp;

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:37.520
they're true because .99 repeating is equal&nbsp;
to one, but these proofs don't show it.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:40.320
you have to keep in mind that&nbsp;
a proof of something is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.400
simply a list of true statements that&nbsp;
ends in the one that you're looking for.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:48.480
i can't just say "one plus one equals&nbsp;
two" "two plus two equals four"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.520
"the fundamental theorem of calculus holds"
these are all true statements, but this is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:03:00.160
a proof of the fundamental theorem of calculus.
you need to show why each step implies the next.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:06.240
since the rules of algebra are so well known ,
in algebraic proofs it's very common to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:07.840
leave off this reasoning.&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.200
but for now, let me make&nbsp;
those implications explicit.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:17.840
so in this slide i'm using this "implies" arrow&nbsp;
to indicate that each line implies the next line.&nbsp;

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.480
i claim that the issue with these&nbsp;
proofs lies in these arrows,&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:24.400
not in the statements themselves.&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:28.160
--these red arrows specifically.
look at the first step on the left.&nbsp;

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:34.480
i multiply 0.99 repeating by 10&nbsp;
and i end up with 9.99 repeating.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:38.240
now, for ordinary finite&nbsp;
decimal expansion numbers,&nbsp;

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:43.680
we know that multiplying by 10 is the same as
just moving the decimal point one to the right.&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:47.280
but how do we know that that&nbsp;
holds for repeated decimals?&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:51.040
after all you could never finish the&nbsp;
computation of multiplying it out.&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:54.880
now again this IS true,&nbsp;
you are allowed to do this.&nbsp;

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.440
but we haven't justified why in this proof.&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.400
the reason that you're allowed to is&nbsp;
actually a fact from calculus, not algebra.&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.080
now, while this step is fine if&nbsp;
you're familiar with calculus,&nbsp;

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:09.280
it's essentially just pushing&nbsp;
a constant inside a limit,&nbsp;

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:13.120
the general population that would be&nbsp;
interested in answering this question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:17.600
"is 0.99 repeating equal to 1?"
we can't assume that they know that.&nbsp;

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.960
if we just use that fact, i think&nbsp;
we're kind of doing them a disservice&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:23.200
because it doesn't really answer their question.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.680
all we've really done is hidden the details&nbsp;
in another thing that they don't understand.&nbsp;

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.040
the problem with the red arrow&nbsp;
on the right is pretty similar,&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:37.280
how do i know that multiplying .33 repeating&nbsp;
times three actually gives .99 repeating?

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:39.920
and as long as we're on&nbsp;
the topic of hiding details&nbsp;

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:41.680
in places you don't understand,&nbsp;

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:46.480
why are you so okay with one-third&nbsp;
being exactly equal to 0.33 repeating?

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:51.600
that sounds kind of just as hard to&nbsp;
prove as 0.99 repeating is equal to 1.&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.080
people seem a lot more okay with that one,
but i don't really think you should be.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:01.600
okay let's start working towards an actual proof.
first let's address what i think is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:06.640
real source of your confusion:
the definition of 0.99 repeating.

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:13.680
what does .99 repeating actually mean?
it is definitely NOT some kind of mystical number&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:16.960
with infinitely many nines&nbsp;
after the decimal point.&nbsp;

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:19.520
that's not a definition.
you have to give a definition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:25.760
in terms of things that are already understood.
so here's how we actually define 0.99 repeating.&nbsp;

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:30.080
we start by defining a sequence.
the first element of the sequence is 0.9,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:36.320
then 0.99, then 0.999 and so on,
where the nth term in the sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:41.920
is just point followed by n 9's.
you can write it for an arbitrary n using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:46.080
this capital sigma fancy math notation,
but that's not really necessary.&nbsp;

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:50.960
remember that for a fixed value of n,
this is just a finite decimal approximation.&nbsp;

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:53.840
it has n digits after the&nbsp;
decimal point and it stops.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:59.840
the definition of 0.99 repeating is just&nbsp;
the number that this sequence approaches,&nbsp;

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:02.800
if there is such a number.
in mathematical terms we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:07.280
say that it is the limit of the sequence,
but it's okay if you're not familiar with limits.&nbsp;

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:10.000
just think "it's the number&nbsp;
that this sequence approaches".&nbsp;

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:12.720
so let's get back to the original question.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:16.880
what is ".99 repeating equals one" even claiming?&nbsp;

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:22.400
all it's saying is that this&nbsp;
sequence 0.9 0.99 0.999 and so on&nbsp;

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:25.200
is approaching a real&nbsp;
number, and that number is 1.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:28.000
well isn't that obvious? you already knew that!

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.400
here's a plot of the first&nbsp;
few terms of the sequence&nbsp;

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:33.440
you can see that it's definitely&nbsp;
going to one pretty quickly.

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:36.880
so at this point do i even&nbsp;
really need to give a proof&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:40.000
now that you understand what this&nbsp;
statement is actually claiming?

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.320
it doesn't seem so mystical anymore, does it?

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:45.440
what it's actually claiming&nbsp;
is just pretty mundane.

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:49.200
but i did say that i will give&nbsp;
a real proof so here it is.&nbsp;

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.680
all we have to show is that that sequence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:56.080
0.9999 approximations is&nbsp;
getting closer and closer to 1.

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:00.000
okay so let's just look at the&nbsp;
difference xn - 1 in absolute value.&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.120
plug in the expression for xn and you see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:07.920
that difference is 10 to the minus n,
or 1 over 10 to the n if you prefer.

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:11.120
i think a lot of people would be&nbsp;
comfortable stopping there and saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:14.880
"okay yeah i see that this goes to zero"
but if you really want to finish the argument&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:19.200
and give the formal definition, then okay.
give me some tolerance epsilon bigger than 0,&nbsp;

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:22.480
then i say well for any n that's&nbsp;
bigger than 1 over epsilon,&nbsp;

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:26.960
we see that that difference is 10 to the minus n,
which is less than 1 over n,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:29.520
which is less than epsilon,
and that's how you would formally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:33.600
show that the sequence approaches one.
but of course you already knew that!

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:36.720
so i hope that really clears things&nbsp;
up for you, so you don't have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.240
irky feeling in your stomach when you&nbsp;
see one of those algebraic proofs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:43.120
still don't really feel&nbsp;
satisfied with the conclusion.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:47.760
all the real hard work is just in understanding&nbsp;
the definition and once you understand that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:50.880
i think it clears right up.
and i do want to address one last thing.

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:54.880
i know that a lot of videos&nbsp;
phrase this as a choice.&nbsp;

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:59.840
what should we choose .99 repeating to be?
and that is completely fair,&nbsp;

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:03.120
you can take the symbols and define&nbsp;
them to mean whatever you want.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:06.800
but notice that this whole process&nbsp;
of defining a repeating decimal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:12.240
didn't really depend on using 0.99.
the real choice here that mathematicians made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:18.400
was not that .99 repeating equals one. the&nbsp;
actual thing that mathematicians decided on was:&nbsp;

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:21.440
what does it mean to add up&nbsp;
an infinite number of things?&nbsp;

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:26.320
so in that sense, we didn't choose&nbsp;
the value of 0.99 repeating to be 1.&nbsp;

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:28.720
we chose the definition of an infinite sum,&nbsp;

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:33.120
and then from that we were able to&nbsp;
derive the value of 0.99 repeating.

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:37.040
and the last thing that i want to&nbsp;
talk about is all those other videos.&nbsp;

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:40.000
now if you just looked at the title of&nbsp;
this video, you might think i'm trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:42.800
to start some kind of flame war,
which i'm definitely not.

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:47.360
i think that each of those videos&nbsp;
is worth watching, and you can learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:51.680
something from each and every one of them.
i just felt like none of those videos really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:55.280
fully removed the source of confusion.
so that's the real reason that&nbsp;&nbsp;

